<section class="section">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Ansible, Salt, and Chef are all capable tools, and plenty of shops run them well.
      OpenVox makes a different set of bets, and if they match how you think about
      infrastructure, nothing else comes close.
    </p>
  </div>

  <vox-grid class="card-grid" min="320px">
    <vox-card heading="Declarative, not procedural.">
      With OpenVox you describe the end state: this package installed, this service
      running, this file with these permissions. The platform figures out how to get
      there on any supported OS, and the same code that builds a system also verifies it.
      You maintain a model of your infrastructure, not a pile of scripts that happened to
      work last time.
    </vox-card>
    <vox-card heading="Continuous enforcement, not one-shot runs.">
      Push-based tools apply changes when someone runs them. The OpenVox agent checks in
      on a schedule, corrects drift automatically, and reports what changed. When someone
      hand-edits a config at 2 a.m., it gets fixed at the next run, and you have a record
      that it happened. At ten nodes that's a convenience. At ten thousand, it's the
      difference between knowing your fleet's state and hoping.
    </vox-card>
    <vox-card heading="A real language for real complexity.">
      The declarative DSL gives you typed parameters, dependency graphs, and reusable
      classes and defined types. Roles and profiles scale from one admin to large teams
      without your codebase collapsing into copy-paste. You get the structure of software
      engineering applied to infrastructure, because that's what it is.
    </vox-card>
    <vox-card heading="A mature ecosystem you already trust.">
      Thousands of battle-tested Forge modules, decades of accumulated operational
      knowledge, and tooling for testing, linting, and CI that treats infrastructure code
      like code. Vox Pupuli alone maintains well over a hundred actively tested modules,
      now validated against OpenVox itself.
    </vox-card>
    <vox-card heading="Built to last.">
      Chef and Puppet both ended up owned by companies with their own priorities. OpenVox
      can't be acquired, sunset, or relicensed out from under you. Its future belongs to
      its users, which means it has one.
    </vox-card>
  </vox-grid>
</section>

